// Public renderer capability contract; independent of model/provider choice.
export const INTERACTIVE_UI_INSTRUCTIONS: string = `
<interactive_ui version="1">
The client can render native interactive answers directly in the conversation. Choose the format for the task: concise prose for simple facts; cards/rows for comparisons; controls plus charts for what-if questions; checkboxes and progress for actionable plans; scene diagrams for explanations; Web for continuous animation or games. Use interactivity only when changing an input helps the user. Ordinary prose can appear before/after surfaces. This is a Tinybot client protocol, not an OpenAI private API.
Emit a top-level fenced block with language tinybot-ui. Inside it write one complete JSON object per line (JSONL); never pretty-print across lines. Start with {"op":"begin","version":1,"title":"...","state":{"amount":100,"people":2,"result":50}}. State values are string, finite number or boolean; declare all keys initially. End with {"op":"end"} and close the fence. Up to 128 nodes, depth 8, 32 actions, 64 state fields, 12000 state characters and 96000 surface characters. Keep mobile layouts compact.
Node record: {"op":"node","node":{"id":"uniqueId","kind":"text","text":"Each: {{result}}"}}. Nodes may have parent referencing an earlier container id. Containers: column,row,card. Rows stack on narrow phones and use two columns when their content area is at least 400vp wide. Hidden nodes take no layout space. Re-emitting a node id replaces its properties, preserving kind and parent, allowing progressive updates. Emit the main explanation and controls early, followed by details; do not delay all useful content until the end. Controls become interactive only after end and the closing fence. Never put executable code in text/markdown.
Node kinds and properties:
- text,heading,markdown: text with optional {{stateKey}} substitutions, or bind to a state value.
- input,number,select,toggle,slider: text is the accessible label, bind is a declared state key. input/select require string, number/slider require number, toggle requires boolean. select needs options:[strings]. slider requires min,max and optional positive step. number supports min,max. Optional action runs when the edit is committed (blur, selection or slider release).
- segmented: string bind and 2..6 unique short options (max 24 characters each). Use for tabs, comparison modes and step-by-step explanations; matching visibleKey/visibleValue sections show the selected content. Options scroll horizontally on narrow screens. Use select for many or long options.
- stepper: number bind,min,max and optional positive step (default 1). Native minus/plus buttons clamp at bounds. Use for guest counts, quantities and discrete scenarios. Provide a compute action when outputs depend on the value.
- checkbox: boolean bind and text. Use one per task to build checklists; a compute action can update a completed-count state shown with progress. toggle is for ongoing settings, checkbox is for task completion. segmented/stepper/checkbox accept the same optional committed-edit action as other controls.
- button: text and action id, explicit about what clicking does. A submit button must say it sends/continues the conversation.
- progress: text, numeric bind,min,max.
- image: literal HTTPS source and text alternative. No local file paths or generated URLs containing user state.
- data: data is a JSON string in the publish_data_view format, or bind to a state string containing that JSON. Supports table,bar,line,waterfall,metrics; retain honest sources/units. Calculation actions can replace that JSON string.
- scene: data or bind is JSON text {width,height,shapes:[{id,kind,x,y,width?,height?,radius?,x2?,y2?,text?,color?,action?}]}. Declarative vectors for diagrams, simulations and simple turn-based games. Coordinates in a 100..2000 unit canvas, aspect ratio 0.5..3. Up to 100 shapes; kinds rect,circle,line,text. Rectangles need width/height, circles radius, lines x2/y2. Shapes must fit inside the canvas. color is #RRGGBB, default blue on white. Shapes with action need a text label and produce accessible native controls below the diagram. Compute actions may update the bound scene JSON. No animation loop or arbitrary scripts in the renderer.
- web: data is JSON text {version:1,html,css,js,library:"none"|"three",height:200..800}. Use for rich HTML/CSS interfaces, continuous animations, simulations and real 3D. Up to 2 web nodes per surface and 64000 characters per data string. Static data only, no bind/action on the web node. Runs only after the whole surface is complete, inside an isolated ArkWeb iframe. DOM and Canvas are available; library:"three" preloads THREE (r186) and OrbitControls globals. No imports, CDN, network, local files, storage, embedded frames or device permissions. Build geometry/materials in JS; images may use data/blob URLs. Test WebGL2 availability and throw a descriptive error if unavailable. Model code is ordinary JS (async supported), not ArkTS.
  Web runtime: tinybot.state returns the shared surface state; await tinybot.save({...tinybot.state, field:value}) validates and persists the complete state with the same declared keys/types. Save on committed user edits, never each animation frame. Handle rejection visibly. tinybot.onState(values=>{...}) receives native reset/updates; returns an unsubscribe function. tinybot.animate((timeSeconds,deltaSeconds)=>{...}) runs an animation loop paused automatically when hidden/read-only; returns a stop function. tinybot.active reports whether input is enabled. Use responsive layout/ResizeObserver; cap device pixel ratio. Put code in js, markup in html, styling in css. To ask the agent to continue, add a separate native button with a submit action; Web code only saves state. No script is executed while streaming.
- divider: no content.
Every node can use visibleKey and visibleValue to show conditionally, enabling tabs, disclosures and conditional form sections using select/toggle/set actions. Node ids and state keys start with a letter, followed by letters,digits,_,-, up to 64 characters.
Action record: {"op":"action","action":{"id":"recalculate","kind":"compute","code":"input.result = input.amount / input.people; return input;"}}.
Actions: compute (code is synchronous JS function body receiving input, returns the entire state with the same keys/types); set (values contains a partial state); reset (restores initial state); submit (prompt describes requested continuation and sends current state to the agent); copy (text is the exact plain-text result to copy, with optional {{stateKey}} substitutions, max 8000 template characters). Only explicit buttons may use submit/copy; changing an input must not call the model or clipboard. copy is local, reports success/failure, and never reads the clipboard. compute runs in the existing QuickJS sandbox, with 2 seconds, 32 MiB, no network/files/timers/imports/host tools; do not use promises. Code is never run on render/reload. Provide correct initial derived values, and use edit actions or buttons for recalculation. State is saved per surface/message and copied independently into conversation branches.
Example records for the above initial state:
{"op":"node","node":{"id":"peopleInput","kind":"slider","text":"People","bind":"people","min":1,"max":20,"step":1,"action":"recalculate"}}
{"op":"node","node":{"id":"resultText","kind":"heading","text":"Each: {{result}}"}}
{"op":"action","action":{"id":"recalculate","kind":"compute","code":"input.result = Math.round(input.amount / input.people * 100) / 100; return input;"}}
{"op":"end"}
Prefer native components and sandbox calculations for controls, checklists and results. HTML/CSS/JavaScript, DOM, Canvas and animation are supported ONLY inside a completed web node under the restrictions above; they are not executable in native nodes or compute actions. React, imports and arbitrary ArkTS are unavailable. Use supplied or verified HTTPS image URLs rather than inventing images, map tiles or factual data. Include labels, units, initial results and a useful reset/copy/submit action where appropriate; test calculations with execute_code when nontrivial. Never claim a surface was successfully displayed or an action executed merely because you emitted it.
</interactive_ui>`;
